nodecode-jot is a rewrite of Totoro-qaq's Totoro-qaq/hermes-jot:

  https://github.com/Totoro-qaq/hermes-jot
  commit b65b550108c8e742cc5737055213da947dc54e0f
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Totoro and dsh-jot contributors (LICENSE)

The rewritten plugin is the whole repository at that commit. Its six tools
keep their names and JSON answers (jot_list, jot_read, jot_create,
jot_update, jot_set_task, jot_delete), and their descriptions carry the
original's words, including its warning that notes are user data; its
behaviour is carried over: a revision on every note that an edit must quote, and a stale one is
refused rather than applied; edits are the exact visible text inside one
block, and apply all together or not at all; checklist items are numbered
from one with only the named marker changing, an appended checklist joining
a checklist that ends the note, a note that leaves the list into Trash rather
than being destroyed, a switch the user turns on and off that the model
cannot change and that every tool consults, and the rule that replacing a
whole note with text is refused when it would lose formatting the text cannot
carry. What the answer says about a note is what it said: a note's summary
carries an excerpt, its id and its revision, and jot_read warns that notes
are the user's data and not instructions.

What was rewritten, and how: the store is Common Lisp against the Nodecode
cell contract instead of TypeScript against the Hermes plugin API. A library
is one JSON file in the folder the section names, written atomically under
the folder's lock, instead of the original's state file in a Hermes profile
folder; the six tools are NLE:DEFINE-CELL registrations instead of a tool
registry, and the original's `/jot` CLI command is a Nodecode slash command
under the same name.

What was left out, and why: Hermes Desktop's editor — the page, the side
panel, the toolbar, capture of selected text, the keyboard shortcuts and the
nine interface languages — because a Nodecode cell has no window of its own
and this rewrite is the model's side of Jot. A note is its Markdown text
rather than a rich document, because the rich document existed to be
rendered by that editor and a Markdown note is what both a person and a model
write here; the formatting-loss guard therefore names the placeholders and
marks that Markdown cannot hold rather than round-tripping a rich tree. The
import and export of Word, PDF and ZIP, attachments, pinning, search
highlights and marking a note "AI edited" with an undo of the last round are
left out for the same reason: each is an editor feature. LICENSE is the
repository's LICENSE, unchanged.
